born with a spinal defect ten-year-old  Eamon sannan says he spent almost half  his life in pain due to my pain  sometimes I couldn't sit in study in  other times I couldn't go out and play  with my friends an end to a suffering is  in sight he's among 30 Yemenis airlifted  out of the capital sana on two un  chartered flights they're receiving  medical treatment in Jordan they've  waited years for I feel glad I got the  opportunity to come here for treatment  but if other Yemenis could come here for  treatment too I would feel better this  is the first time in more than three  years that Yemenis have been allowed to  fly from the capital Sena for medical  treatment abroad Senna is controlled by  the Houthis but Yemeni airspace is  controlled and a blockade imposed by the  saudi-led coalition at war with the  Houthis for the past five years the  blockade has made it challenging for  humanitarian organizations to assist  Yemenis in need the UN and the World  Health Organization or whu-oh joined to  negotiate with the saudi-led coalition  to make the emergency evacuations  possible the w-h-o says the war has  shattered Yemen's health care system  more than half of all hospitals aren't  fully operational many medical workers  have fled abroad and the blockade has  created a shortage of medical supplies  when I'm safe we were very depressed and  annoyed because you have a patient you  can't help we were willing to sell  whatever we had to get treatment in  Yemen but in Yemen there are no  specialized centers for treatment samira  el mar has already had the first of  several operations needed to reconstruct  her ear injured in an accident  she says she spent almost six years  feeling self-conscious about her injury  like I was not feeling good and when I  went to school I was feeling shy because  my ear was deformed the w-h-o hopes  these medevac flights will be the first  of many but the need is great an  estimated 30,000 Yemenis are waiting and  praying for it  take it 